// EXPECT: E0223 — the pointer *difference* `p - q` is deferred (ADR-0064 §5)
//
// Pointer offset landed in ADR-0064: `p + n`, `n + p` and `p - n` all work, element-scaled. What is
// *not* in it is `p - q`, the distance between two pointers — its result is a count of elements, so it
// must divide the byte distance by the element stride, and the stride is layout that ADR-0017 §5 keeps
// out of `jr-mir` (the back ends scale a `Projection::Index`; `jr-mir` never handles a size). So `p -
// q` needs a new MIR node or a layout query the mid-end does not have — a decision of its own, and one
// a bump allocator (which only advances a pointer) does not force. Until its wave, it is E0223: the
// operator does not fit these operands, which is exactly what E0223 says.
//
// `n - p` — an integer minus a pointer — is a *separate* refusal with its own message ("cannot
// subtract a pointer from `s64`", since the distance is `p - n`, the other order). It is the same code
// by design; this file pins the `p - q` case, which is the one the deferral is about.
main :: () {
    a: [4]s64;
    p := *a[0];
    q := *a[2];
    d := p - q;
    a[0] = d;
}
